&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;1. Introduction&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Modern web applications frequently need to know whether an element is visible to the user.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Load an image only when the user approaches it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Start an animation when a section appears.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Load more products when the user reaches the bottom.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Track whether an advertisement was viewed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Play a video only while it is visible.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One traditional solution is to listen for the &lt;strong&gt;scroll&lt;/strong&gt; event and continuously calculate element positions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React applications can instead use the browser's &lt;strong&gt;Intersection Observer API.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It provides a simple way to react when an element enters or leaves a particular area, usually the browser viewport.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;2. What Is Intersection Observer?&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Intersection Observer is a browser Web API that allows JavaScript to detect when one element intersects with another element or the viewport.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In simple terms:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Intersection Observer answers the question: "Is this element currently entering, inside, or leaving the visible area?"&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;User scrolls
     ↓
Element approaches viewport
     ↓
Intersection detected
     ↓
Callback executes
     ↓
React performs an action
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It is useful because developers do not need to repeatedly calculate an element's position during scrolling.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;3. How It Works&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The basic Intersection Observer flow is:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Create observer
      ↓
Select target element
      ↓
Observe target
      ↓
Element intersects viewport
      ↓
Callback executes
      ↓
Perform required action
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Basic JavaScript:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IntersectionObserver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isIntersecting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;observe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;element&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;isIntersecting&lt;/strong&gt; tells us whether the target is currently intersecting the observer's root.&lt;br&gt;
In React, the same API can be connected to a DOM element using &lt;strong&gt;useRef()&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;4. Basic React Example&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A simple React implementation looks like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ref&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setVisible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IntersectionObserver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setVisible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isIntersecting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ref&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;observe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ref&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;disconnect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The reference is attached to the element:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ref&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ref&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Visible"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Not Visible"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What happens?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the element enters the viewport:&lt;br&gt;
isIntersecting = true&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When it leaves:&lt;br&gt;
isIntersecting = false&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React can then change the UI, start an animation, request data, or perform another action.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;5. Important Intersection Observer Options&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two options are particularly important.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;threshold&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;threshold determines how much of the target should intersect before the observer reports the threshold crossing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;{ threshold: 0 }&lt;br&gt;
Useful when detecting an element as it begins entering the viewport.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;{ threshold: 0.5 }&lt;br&gt;
Useful when approximately half of the element should be visible before taking action.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;{ threshold: 1 }&lt;br&gt;
Useful when the target needs to be completely visible for the threshold condition.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Multiple thresholds are also possible:&lt;br&gt;
{ threshold: [0, 0.5, 1] }&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;rootMargin&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;rootMargin changes the effective observation area.&lt;br&gt;
Example:&lt;br&gt;
{ rootMargin: "200px" }&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This can detect an element before it actually reaches the visible viewport.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is particularly useful for preloading.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Image is 200px away
       ↓
Observer detects image
       ↓
Start loading
       ↓
User reaches image
       ↓
Image is ready
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;6. Practical Use Cases&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Intersection Observer is most useful when the requirement is related to &lt;strong&gt;visibility&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Common applications
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lazy Loading&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Load images or content only when they approach the viewport.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Scroll Animations&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Start an animation when a section becomes visible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Infinite Scrolling&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Load the next set of records when the user reaches the bottom.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Analytics&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Track when users actually see a particular section or advertisement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Video Control&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Play or pause a video based on its visibility.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;On-Demand Content&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Fetch expensive data only when the user reaches the relevant section.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;7. Practical Example: Lazy Loading&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine an e-commerce application containing hundreds of product images.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Loading every image immediately is unnecessary because the user may never scroll far enough to see many of them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Intersection Observer can detect when an image approaches the viewport.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IntersectionObserver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isIntersecting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setLoadImage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;disconnect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;rootMargin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"200px"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The process becomes:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Page loads
   ↓
Image is far away
   ↓
Do nothing
   ↓
User scrolls
   ↓
Image approaches viewport
   ↓
Observer triggers
   ↓
Load image
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where can you use this?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;E-commerce product lists&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Image galleries&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;News websites&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Social feeds&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Portfolio websites&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For basic image lazy loading, native HTML also provides:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Intersection Observer is more useful when custom loading behavior is required.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;8. Practical Example: Scroll Animations&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Modern websites often animate sections as users scroll.&lt;br&gt;
For example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Section outside viewport
        ↓
Opacity: 0

User scrolls
        ↓
Section becomes visible
        ↓
Opacity: 1
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;React can use Intersection Observer to add a CSS class.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IntersectionObserver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isIntersecting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setVisible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;disconnect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;CSS can then handle the animation:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.section&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;opacity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;transform&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;translateY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;30px&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.section.visible&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;opacity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;transform&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;translateY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;*&lt;em&gt;Suitable for : *&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Landing pages&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Portfolio websites&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Corporate websites&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Product pages&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Marketing websites&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The important idea is to let &lt;strong&gt;Intersection Observer detect visibility and CSS handle the animation&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;9. Practical Example: Infinite Scrolling&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Infinite scrolling is another common use case.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine a product listing:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Product 1
Product 2
Product 3
...
Product 20

       Sentinel
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A small element called a sentinel is placed at the bottom.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the sentinel enters the viewport:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Sentinel visible
      ↓
Request next page
      ↓
Receive products
      ↓
Add products
      ↓
Sentinel moves downward
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IntersectionObserver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isIntersecting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nf"&gt;loadMoreProducts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;rootMargin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"300px"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where can you use it?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Social media feeds&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;E-commerce listings&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Search results&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;News feeds&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Video feeds&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In production, also manage loading states so multiple API requests are not triggered simultaneously.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;10. Practical Example: Visibility Analytics&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Suppose a page contains an advertisement.&lt;br&gt;
Simply rendering the advertisement does not necessarily mean the user saw it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Intersection Observer can detect when it becomes visible.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IntersectionObserver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isIntersecting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nf"&gt;trackAdView&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;disconnect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;threshold&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.5&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Here, the application can use a visibility threshold appropriate to its analytics requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same approach can be used for:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Advertisement impressions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Product visibility&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Article sections&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Marketing content&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Feature usage&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;11. Practical Example: Video Control&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Intersection Observer can also control media based on visibility.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Video enters viewport
       ↓
Play

Video leaves viewport
       ↓
Pause
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IntersectionObserver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isIntersecting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;video&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;play&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;video&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;pause&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;threshold&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.5&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This can be useful for:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Short-video feeds&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Product videos&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Educational websites&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Social media interfaces&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Browser autoplay policies still need to be considered when automatically playing videos.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;12. Reusable Custom Hook&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If Intersection Observer is used throughout a React project, creating a custom hook can avoid repeated code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A simplified hook can look like:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useIntersectionObserver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;options&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ref&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setVisible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ref&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

        &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IntersectionObserver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setVisible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isIntersecting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;options&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

        &lt;span class="n"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;observe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ref&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;observer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;disconnect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;options&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ref&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A component can then use:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ref&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useIntersectionObserver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;threshold&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.2&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This is useful when many components need the same visibility behavior.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Benefits&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Less duplicated code&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Easier maintenance&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reusable logic&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Cleaner React components&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;13. Common Mistakes&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Forgetting Cleanup&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Always disconnect the observer when the component is removed.&lt;br&gt;
return () =&amp;gt; observer.disconnect();&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Creating Unnecessary Observers&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
If multiple elements need to be monitored, one observer can often observe multiple targets.&lt;br&gt;
observer.observe(element1);&lt;br&gt;
observer.observe(element2);&lt;br&gt;
observer.observe(element3);&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Triggering API Calls Repeatedly&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
For infinite scrolling or data loading, protect against duplicate requests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Already loading?&lt;br&gt;
      ↓&lt;br&gt;
Yes → Don't request again&lt;br&gt;
No  → Start request&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Using Intersection Observer for Everything&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
It is designed for intersection and visibility.&lt;br&gt;
It is not a replacement for:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;click&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;mousemove&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;keydown&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Drag and drop&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;General event handling&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;14. Performance Tips&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use rootMargin intelligently&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;br&gt;
rootMargin: "200px"&lt;br&gt;
can provide extra time for content to load before it becomes visible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Disconnect one-time observers&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
If an action only needs to happen once:&lt;br&gt;
observer.disconnect();&lt;br&gt;
or:&lt;br&gt;
observer.unobserve(element);&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Avoid unnecessary React state updates&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Only update state when the visibility change affects the application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Combine it with other optimizations&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Intersection Observer works well alongside:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Image optimization&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pagination&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Code splitting&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Native lazy loading&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;API caching&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Virtualized lists&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;15. Stats&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;**Browser Support: **Intersection Observer is supported by approximately 97% of global web users. 
Source: &lt;a href="https://caniuse.com/intersectionobserver" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://caniuse.com/intersectionobserver&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Multiple Targets:&lt;/strong&gt; One IntersectionObserver instance can observe multiple target elements, reducing the need to create separate observers for each element. 
Source: &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/IntersectionObserver/observe" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/IntersectionObserver/observe&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Threshold Configuration:&lt;/strong&gt; Developers can configure a single threshold or an array of thresholds to detect different intersection ratios. 
Source: &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/IntersectionObserver/IntersectionObserver" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/IntersectionObserver/IntersectionObserver&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;**Asynchronous Observation: **The API reports intersection changes asynchronously, helping developers avoid repeatedly calculating element positions during scrolling. 
Source: &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Intersection_Observer_API" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Intersection_Observer_API" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Intersection_Observer_API&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;**Native Browser API: **Intersection Observer is a browser feature and does not require a React-specific library. Source: &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Intersection_Observer_API" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Intersection_Observer_API&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;16. Interesting Facts&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Framework Independent: Intersection Observer is a native JavaScript API that works with React, Vue, Angular, and plain JavaScript.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Multiple Elements: A single observer instance can monitor multiple elements, making it useful for lists, cards, and page sections.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Early Detection: The rootMargin option can detect elements before they enter the viewport, enabling content preloading.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Multiple Use Cases: Beyond animations, Intersection Observer supports lazy loading, analytics, infinite scrolling, and media playback control.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Native Lazy Loading: For simple image lazy loading, the browser's built-in loading="lazy" attribute may be sufficient. Intersection Observer is more useful when custom application logic is needed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;17. FAQs&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What is Intersection Observer?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
It is a browser API that detects when an element enters or leaves an observation area such as the viewport.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Is Intersection Observer part of React?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
No. It is a browser API that can be integrated into React using hooks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What is isIntersecting?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
It indicates whether the observed element is currently intersecting the observer's root.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What is threshold?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
It controls the intersection ratio at which the observer reports a threshold crossing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What is rootMargin?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
It changes the effective observation area and can allow detection before an element actually enters the viewport.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Can Intersection Observer be used for infinite scrolling?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Yes. A sentinel at the bottom of a list can trigger the next API request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Can it be used for animations?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Yes. It can add a CSS class when an element becomes visible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Can it lazy-load images?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Yes. However, native loading="lazy" may be sufficient for basic image loading.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Can one observer monitor multiple elements?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Yes. A single observer can monitor multiple targets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Should I create a custom hook?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
If your project uses Intersection Observer in several components, a custom hook can make the code cleaner and reusable.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;18. Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Intersection Observer provides a simple solution for a common frontend requirement:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;"Do something when this element becomes visible."&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of continuously calculating element positions during scrolling, developers can let the browser report intersection changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The basic React pattern is:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;useRef()
   ↓
DOM element
   ↓
IntersectionObserver
   ↓
Intersection detected
   ↓
React state/action
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Its most common applications include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Lazy loading&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Scroll animations&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Infinite scrolling&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Analytics&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Video control&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;On-demand API requests&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The two options developers should understand first are:&lt;br&gt;
threshold&lt;br&gt;
rootMargin&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For reusable applications, a custom React hook can further simplify the implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Intersection Observer is therefore an important API for React developers who want to build interfaces that load, animate, or react to content based on what the user is actually seeing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;strong&gt;Quotes&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;“The best optimization is often the work you don't perform until it is actually needed.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;“Great web experiences deliver the right content at the right moment.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;“Performance is not only about doing things faster; it is also about avoiding unnecessary work.”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published on &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/ai-couldnt-see-bug-working-examples-from-clay-ui-changed-4ilef/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;LinkedIn&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted readers to turn the pages of &lt;a href="https://theartofai.behavio.one" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The Art of AI&lt;/a&gt; like a real book. On my laptop the animation looked right. On my phone, parts of the page flashed black.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Several attempts to fix it with Claude Opus 5.5 failed. Working examples found through &lt;a href="https://clayui.behavio.one" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Clay UI&lt;/a&gt; pointed to a better approach, which we proved in an Android emulator and checked on my phone. Opus built the first working prototype, and Fable 5.2 settled it and brought it to the version that is live today.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This article is about how we got from plausible fixes to a page turn that works, and what made the difference.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A book that should turn like paper
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Art of AI is an illustrated book of short reflections on artificial intelligence, published as a website. I prepared the code for the page turn myself, so that a page lifts at the corner, curls softly and settles on the next one. When it broke on my phone, I asked Claude Opus 5.5 to help me fix it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I saw on the phone
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During a turn, parts of the page went black. Often the whole lower half vanished behind a straight edge under the illustration, and sometimes stripes ran across the paper. A few frames later the page was fine again, so it looked like a flicker. On a computer none of this happened.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a screenshot I took on my phone in the middle of a turn. The text is cut off and the left half of the page is simply missing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ff4jo3q8lmsbyyst4fbau.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ff4jo3q8lmsbyyst4fbau.jpg" alt="Phone screenshot in the middle of a page turn: the left half of the page is missing" width="800" height="1631"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why the AI kept failing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The AI failed several times before it got anywhere. Its first report said: "I couldn't find a single flash frame." It had checked in a desktop browser set to a phone's screen size. That copies the size of a phone, but not its small graphics memory, and that is where the fault lived. Reading the code did not reveal the cause.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My own assumption didn't help. I said it had not flickered before, but the older version broke the same way in an Android emulator. There was no recent change to blame.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The older version in the Android emulator. Stripes and broken paper run across both pages, with the same library and the same pages as before.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fcgqw0o4wi3ukckcqdruw.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fcgqw0o4wi3ukckcqdruw.png" alt="The older version in the Android emulator with stripes across both pages" width="720" height="800"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The emulator gave shaky answers too: the same unchanged build broke in 17%, 27% and 53% of frames on three runs. So the AI guessed and tested one suspect after another, from shadows and the paper texture to the illustrations and browser hints. None of them fixed it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Frames from one emulator recording of the live site. Black lower halves, half-drawn pages and stripes appear in different frames of the same turns.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fsny82p8oatv6sltwi0rv.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fsny82p8oatv6sltwi0rv.jpg" alt="Frames from an emulator recording with black lower halves and half-drawn pages" width="800" height="465"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The way out: working examples from Clay UI
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The AI failed again and again; the way out came from the patterns in &lt;a href="https://clayui.behavio.one" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Clay UI&lt;/a&gt;, which is open source on &lt;a href="https://github.com/behavio1/clay-ui" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;. Clay UI searches catalogs of ready-made interface components and downloads their source code. For a page-flip book it found two working examples: page-flip from Kokonut UI and book-flip from Avara.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;  &lt;iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/itN9cm4diZ8" width="710" height="399"&gt;
  &lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What &lt;a href="https://clayui.behavio.one" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Clay UI&lt;/a&gt; does, in under 40 seconds of clay: proven components from real libraries, shaped to your style. &lt;a href="https://youtu.be/itN9cm4diZ8" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Watch the film&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They paid off first on the buttons. Ours switched off during every turn and faded to 42% opacity. Kokonut keeps its buttons on and ignores extra clicks in code, and we did the same.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The controls frame by frame during page turns. In the left column, before the fix, Previous and Next fade out on every turn.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fev5wa7s4i27cddwgzgp2.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fev5wa7s4i27cddwgzgp2.jpg" alt="Previous and Next buttons frame by frame during page turns, before and after the fix" width="780" height="784"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On the pages, the examples changed the question. It stopped being "what is wrong with my page" and became "what do working page turns do differently". In plain words: my version made the phone redraw the whole page again and again during a turn. The working examples draw the page once and then only move that finished image.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For developers: Avara turns a page with a 3D rotation, which the graphics chip can apply to an already painted layer. My library cut the page to a new shape in every frame, so the browser had to repaint it 60 times a second. With a paper texture and a large illustration on every page, the phone ran out of graphics memory, and each missing tile showed as black.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A quick copy of Avara's approach, with rigid pages, removed the black halves, but the page turned like a board. I wanted the soft curl. The patterns gave us the direction; proving it and building it were still ahead.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The key: patterns plus emulation and tests
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The patterns inspired a much stricter process. Opus 5.5 limited Chrome's graphics memory in the emulator to 96 MB, a deliberately low setting that imitates a phone under pressure. From then on the bug appeared every time, just as on my phone. Then it ran a control: the same library with plain pages, under the same limit, turned 65 frames without a broken one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The control run: the same library with plain pages, recorded frame by frame. Every turn draws cleanly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fj4qsmynjppg0dhogphnl.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fj4qsmynjppg0dhogphnl.jpg" alt="Control test comparing the page-flip library alone with the full book pages" width="800" height="556"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So in this test the library alone held up. The difference was my heavy pages, painted again in every frame. A memory reading showed that my page held 118 MB of graphics memory even at rest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The drawing below shows the difference in what the phone has to do in every frame of a turn.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F1g63zb3qgxssbdq556vq.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F1g63zb3qgxssbdq556vq.png" alt="Diagram: repainting the whole page every frame versus moving a finished image" width="799" height="324"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The tests also showed why guessing never worked. Removing one ingredient made the page lighter, but it was still painted again in every frame.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fkqbdindihksre7e9fhub.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fkqbdindihksre7e9fhub.png" alt="Chart of broken frames per variant in the emulator tests" width="800" height="396"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Fable 5.2 settles it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Opus 5.5, guided by the Clay UI patterns, found the direction and built a first prototype: the turn drawn from finished pictures of the pages. In the emulator, under the same low memory limit, we checked 949 animation frames in a row and found no broken one. Fable 5.2 settled the matter and brought it to the version that is live today.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fable measured the graphics memory and wrote its own page-curl engine on a canvas, a drawing surface inside the browser. A script photographs each of the 86 pages in a fixed phone layout. During a turn the canvas finds the fold line, keeps the front of the page flat and mirrors the back of the paper over the fold, so nothing on the page is painted again. When I opened it on my phone, I wrote back: "It looks very good, I can't see anything breaking."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The comparison below comes from the emulator with the 96 MB limit. The top two rows are the old version and the bottom two the canvas version, turning the same pages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fsgf72h8gl983pdlfmp9g.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fsgf72h8gl983pdlfmp9g.jpg" alt="Before and after: the page turn on the phone" width="800" height="456"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The last details
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first version showed a picture of the page during a turn and switched back to live text when the turn ended. Every small difference between the two showed as a jump. Fable 5.2 fixed them one by one:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The chapter 9 title sat on three lines during a turn and on two after it. The line breaks are now recorded with the pictures and forced on the live page.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;After each turn the text got a little thinner and the illustration twitched. The pictures had been taken on a Mac, which draws text bolder than a phone. They are now taken without that, and the picture stays on screen after the turn too, so there is no switch to see.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A zoomed illustration jumped at the end of closing. It now lands on the picture's exact spot, with matching corners and shadow.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A page now has to finish turning before the next turn can start.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What made the difference
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On its own, AI kept failing on this. Working patterns from &lt;a href="https://clayui.behavio.one" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Clay UI&lt;/a&gt; showed what good page turns do differently, and a detailed process of emulation and tests turned that idea into proof. Opus 5.5 found the direction with them, and Fable 5.2 finished the job. The Clay UI source is on &lt;a href="https://github.com/behavio1/clay-ui" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The last part was mine. Our automated checks missed a title that changes lines and text that gets a little thinner. I caught them by reading my own book on my own phone. You can turn its pages at &lt;a href="https://theartofai.behavio.one" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;theartofai.behavio.one&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fdlkfudgq7gi3fqk5ozxb.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fdlkfudgq7gi3fqk5ozxb.jpg" alt="About the author: Marcin Tabędzki, author of The Art of AI, builds Clay UI and AI tools at Behavio One" width="800" height="227"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About the author:&lt;/strong&gt; Marcin Tabędzki, author of &lt;a href="https://theartofai.behavio.one" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The Art of AI&lt;/a&gt;, builds &lt;a href="https://clayui.behavio.one" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Clay UI&lt;/a&gt; and AI tools at Behavio One. Find me on LinkedIn: &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/marcin-tabedzki/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;linkedin.com/in/marcin-tabedzki&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every design system has a gap between what it offers and what gets written. You can argue about that gap in the abstract for a long time. I wanted to look at it in real code, change by change, so I took a popular open-source document-sharing product and replayed its last hundred merged changes against the components and tokens it has today.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is what I found, how I checked it, and why the product stays anonymous.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The method
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The idea is simple. Take a repository's recent history and, for each merged change, ask one question: did this change add something the codebase already had?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For each change, the check looks only at the lines that change added, not the whole file it touched. It compares them with the components and design tokens the repository declares. Two kinds of finding count.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Hand-built UI where a component already existed: a button element styled by hand where the codebase has &lt;code&gt;Button&lt;/code&gt;, a pulsing grey block where it has a skeleton loader component.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A raw value exactly equal to a token or scale step that already existed: a pixel size that is a step on the spacing scale, a colour that is the value of a declared colour token.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One rule matters more than the rest. A finding of hand-built UI is kept only if the component already existed when the change was made. Building a button by hand before anyone had written &lt;code&gt;Button&lt;/code&gt; is not a mistake. It is how components start.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The replay runs on a temporary copy of each commit and never touches the working repository. Nothing is uploaded, and no AI model is involved in the check itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What it found
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Of the last hundred merged changes, all but a handful touched the product's UI source.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;4 of them added hand-built UI where the codebase already had the component. The most common case by far was a hand-built loading placeholder where the skeleton loader component existed. Raw buttons and inputs, and a new toggle built from scratch where a &lt;code&gt;Toggle&lt;/code&gt; component already existed, made up the rest.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;9 of them wrote a raw value exactly equal to an existing token or scale step: white and near-black colours that were already tokens, pixel sizes that were already steps on the scale, z-index values that matched a declared layer.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The check also turned up values that matched nothing: widths, font sizes and radii that sat between two steps of the scale. Those are a different kind of finding. They are not a bypass, because there was no token to bypass. They are a question for the team: is this a new step, or a value that should round to an existing one?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There were contrast findings too, text colours on backgrounds below the WCAG contrast minimum. I am leaving those aside here. They are worth fixing, but they are not about reuse.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How I checked it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A count like this is only worth publishing if each finding holds up. So every finding in this note was checked by hand against the source: the line the change added, the component or token it was compared with, and whether that component existed at the time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is the step I will not skip. A check that names a pattern can be wrong, and the cost of a wrong claim about someone else's code is much higher than the cost of a missing one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I have replayed other products the same way. Their notes have not yet been read by hand, so I am not quoting their numbers. What I can say is that the results varied a lot between products, which is itself useful: this is not a fixed rate of sloppiness. It depends on how visible the components are and how a team works.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why the product is anonymous
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The product is open source, the history is public, and anyone could run the same replay. I still will not name it, for three reasons.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;These findings describe how code gets written under time pressure, by people and, more and more, by coding agents. They are not a verdict on the team, and a name invites that reading.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A wrong claim with a company's name on it is the real risk, to them and to me. Anonymising by default removes it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If a team wants to see the full note about their own code, the right way is to offer it through their public channels, and let them decide what happens next.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I describe the findings in plain words and never paste their code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What it says about agents
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of these changes is labelled as written by an agent, and I make no claim that they were. What links them to agents is the shape of the mistake.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In an October benchmark of Saer, Codex hand-built UI the codebase already had a component for in 22 runs without access to the design system, and in 5 with it, out of the same number of runs on each side. The patterns were the same ones this replay found: loading placeholders, buttons, inputs and empty states built fresh beside the component that already existed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An agent writes from what it can see. A person in a hurry does the same. As more changes are written with agents, the number of chances to rebuild something goes up, and so does the value of a check that asks the reuse question on every change.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I take from it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Replay history before you argue about drift. A hundred real changes say more than any amount of opinion.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Judge only the lines a change adds, and only against components that existed when it was made.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Read every finding by hand before you publish a count.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Keep values that match nothing apart from values that bypass a token.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Describe the pattern, not the team.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The replay is part of Saer, the tool I am building to keep coding agents on the design system a codebase already has. You can request early access at saer.app.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Introduction
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React is a popular JavaScript library used to build interactive user interfaces. One of its most powerful features is &lt;strong&gt;Hooks&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hooks allow functional components to use React features such as state management, side effects, and shared data without writing class components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Among the most commonly used React Hooks are:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;useState()&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;useEffect()&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;useContext()&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's understand each hook, how it works, and when to use it.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  1. useState() – Managing Component State
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  What is useState?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useState()&lt;/code&gt; is a React Hook that allows us to create and manage state inside a functional component.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;State is data that can change over time. Whenever the state changes, React re-renders the component to display the updated information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Syntax
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;initialValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;state&lt;/code&gt;: Stores the current value.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;setState&lt;/code&gt;: Updates the state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;initialValue&lt;/code&gt;: Defines the initial state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Example: Counter Application
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Counter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

            &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
                &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Increment&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

            &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
                &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Decrement&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Counter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  How does it work?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Initially, the count value is &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;When the user clicks Increment, &lt;code&gt;setCount()&lt;/code&gt; updates the state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React detects the state update.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React re-renders the component with the new value.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Real-world use cases:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Counter applications&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Form input handling&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Toggle buttons&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Shopping cart quantities&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Showing and hiding components&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  2. useEffect() – Handling Side Effects
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  What is useEffect?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useEffect()&lt;/code&gt; is a React Hook used to perform side effects in functional components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A side effect is an operation that interacts with something outside the component's rendering process.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Examples include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Fetching data from an API.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Setting up timers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Updating document titles.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Subscribing to external events.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Syntax
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Side-effect logic&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Cleanup logic (optional)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dependencies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  Understanding the dependency array
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The dependency array controls when the effect runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Without a dependency array&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Component rendered"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Runs after every render.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Empty dependency array&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Component mounted"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Runs after the initial render.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. With dependencies&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Count changed"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Runs after the initial render and whenever &lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt; changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Example: Fetching API Data
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;axios&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'axios'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Products&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;products&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setProducts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;axios&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://dummyjson.com/products'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
                &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setProducts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;products&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;products&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
                &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h3&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
                    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
                &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Products&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  How does it work?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The component renders.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useEffect()&lt;/code&gt; executes after the initial render.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Axios sends a request to the API.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The API returns product data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;setProducts()&lt;/code&gt; updates the state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React re-renders the component with the products.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Real-world use cases:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Fetching API data&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Authentication checks&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Event listeners&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Timers and intervals&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Synchronizing with external systems&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  3. useContext() – Sharing Data Between Components
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  What is useContext?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useContext()&lt;/code&gt; is a React Hook that allows components to access shared data without passing props manually through multiple component levels.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This problem is commonly known as &lt;strong&gt;Prop Drilling&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Understanding Prop Drilling
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine we have three components:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;App (Parent)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Home (Child)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Profile (Grandchild)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If App contains user information and Profile needs that information, we might have to pass props through Home even when Home doesn't need the data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Context API helps solve this problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Step 1: Create a Context
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;createContext&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;MyContext&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  Step 2: Create a Provider
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;MyContext&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'./MyContext'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Home&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'./Home'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Raja"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;age&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;22&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MyContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Provider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setUser&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Home&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MyContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Provider&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  Step 3: Access Context in Another Component
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useContext&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;MyContext&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'./MyContext'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Profile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setUser&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MyContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;age&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

            &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
                &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;age&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;23&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
                &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Update&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Age&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Profile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  How does it work?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;createContext()&lt;/code&gt; creates a context.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The Provider makes data available to its descendant components.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The &lt;code&gt;value&lt;/code&gt; prop contains the shared data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useContext()&lt;/code&gt; accesses that data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Components can use the shared state and update it through its setter.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Real-world use cases:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;User authentication information&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Shopping cart data&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Theme switching (dark/light mode)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Language preferences&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Global application settings&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  4. Difference Between These Three Hooks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Hook&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Main Purpose&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Example&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;useState&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Manage component state&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Counter&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;useEffect&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Handle side effects&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;API fetching&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;useContext&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Share data between components&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Shopping cart&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  5. Can We Use These Hooks Together?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Absolutely! These hooks are often used together in real-world React applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, in an e-commerce application:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useState()&lt;/code&gt; manages the cart items.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useEffect()&lt;/code&gt; fetches products from an API.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useContext()&lt;/code&gt; shares cart information between Product and Cart components.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each hook has a different responsibility, but together they help build scalable and interactive applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Conclusion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Hooks make functional components more powerful and easier to manage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Understanding &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;useContext&lt;/code&gt; is an important step toward becoming a React developer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A commenter on Reddit looked at one of my charts for about ten seconds and found a bug I had shipped to everyone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The chart was a combo chart: revenue as bars on the left axis, profit margin as a line on the right axis. Pretty standard dashboard stuff. Their question was simple: "where is zero on the right axis?"&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It wasn't where the bars started.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What went wrong
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two axes on one chart means two sets of tick labels sharing one set of grid lines. My first version got half of that right. The right axis picked the same number of ticks as the left, each one a round number, so every label sat neatly on a grid line.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What it never did was force the two zeros onto the same line.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Take revenue from $0 to $62K and a margin between −10% and 35%:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Left axis: &lt;code&gt;0, 20K, 40K, 60K, 80K&lt;/code&gt; (five lines)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Right axis: five lines, round step, must cover −10% to 35%, so: &lt;code&gt;−20%, 0%, 20%, 40%, 60%&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Look at the second line. The right axis says 0%, the left says $20K. The bars stand on the bottom line, but the margin's zero floats one line above it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now give one month a −5% margin. The dot lands &lt;em&gt;above&lt;/em&gt; the bars' baseline. Anyone skimming the chart reads it as "slightly positive". It's a loss.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every label was a round number, every tick sat on a grid line, and the chart was still wrong.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fyxpif5c3lws0a3jlk0yi.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fyxpif5c3lws0a3jlk0yi.png" alt="The same data before and after: on the left, the right axis puts 0% on the $20K line, so the negative months float above the bars' baseline. On the right, both zeros share one line and the negative months dip below it." width="799" height="275"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The fix
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the chart starts at zero, both axes now get &lt;strong&gt;the same number of steps below zero and the same number above it&lt;/strong&gt;, the most either axis needs. Zero becomes one shared grid line, by construction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The left axis keeps its own round step. The right axis tries a few candidates (1, 2 or 5 times a power of ten) and keeps the one with the best score:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// fewer lines is calmer, but an axis its values barely use squashes the line&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;filled&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;hi&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;step&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;below&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;above&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;score&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;below&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;above&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;filled&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The first term prefers fewer grid lines. The second punishes a step so large that the data only fills a sliver of the axis, which would flatten the line into a near-straight squiggle.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Same data, after the fix:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Left: &lt;code&gt;−20K, 0, 20K, 40K, 60K, 80K&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Right: &lt;code&gt;−10%, 0%, 10%, 20%, 30%, 40%&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both zeros sit on one line, and the −5% month now dips below it, where a loss belongs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two smaller changes came with it:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The solid grid line moved from the bottom of the chart to zero, where the bars actually stand.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;alignZero={false}&lt;/code&gt; turns it off, for the rare chart where two independent domains really are what you want.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I took from it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tests wouldn't have caught this. Every output was a valid, round, evenly spaced set of ticks. The bug only exists when you &lt;em&gt;read&lt;/em&gt; the chart the way a person does, comparing one axis against the other.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So: show your charts to someone with negative numbers in their data. Mine all had happy, positive margins.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The component is free and MIT if you want to poke at it: &lt;a href="https://dashral.com/components/combo-chart?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_campaign=zero-baseline" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://dashral.com/components/combo-chart?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_campaign=zero-baseline&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


  Localizing Agri-tech: Building a Multilingual Next.js App for Rural India
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you build an app for a farmer in Nashik or a trader in Haveri using standard English UI patterns, you’ve already lost. In the Indian agri-tech landscape, English isn't just a barrier; it’s a trust-killer. When we started building &lt;a href="https://khetisetu.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;KhetiSetu&lt;/a&gt;, we knew that supporting Hindi, Marathi, and Kannada wasn't a "nice-to-have" feature—it was the core product requirement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The challenge with multilingual apps in the Indian context isn't just swapping strings. It’s about handling varying script densities, managing "Hinglish" terminology that farmers actually use, and ensuring that a low-end Android device on a 3G connection doesn't choke on massive translation bundles.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is how we architected our Next.js App Router to handle high-performance localization across three major Indian languages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The Architecture: Middleware-based Routing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We opted for a sub-path routing strategy (e.g., &lt;code&gt;/hi/mandi&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;/kn/mandi&lt;/code&gt;). This is superior to cookie-only localization because it allows farmers to share links via WhatsApp that preserve the language context. If a farmer in Karnataka shares a crop listing with a peer, the recipient should see it in Kannada immediately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In Next.js, we leverage the &lt;code&gt;middleware.ts&lt;/code&gt; to intercept requests and redirect users based on their preferred language or geographic IP if no locale is present.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// middleware.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;NextResponse&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'next/server'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;NextRequest&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'next/server'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;match&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;matchLocale&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@formatjs/intl-localematcher'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Negotiator&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'negotiator'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;locales&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'en'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'hi'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'mr'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'kn'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;defaultLocale&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'hi'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getLocale&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;NextRequest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;negotiatorHeaders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;forEach&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;negotiatorHeaders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;languages&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Negotiator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;negotiatorHeaders&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;languages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;matchLocale&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;languages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;locales&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;defaultLocale&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;middleware&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;NextRequest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pathname&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;nextUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pathname&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pathnameIsMissingLocale&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;locales&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;every&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;locale&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pathname&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;startsWith&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`/${locale}/`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pathname&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`/${locale}`&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pathnameIsMissingLocale&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;locale&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getLocale&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;NextResponse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;redirect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`/${locale}${pathname.startsWith('/') ? '' : '/'}${pathname}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;config&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;matcher&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'/((?!api|_next/static|_next/image|favicon.ico).*)'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  Solving the "Dictionary Debt"
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In a marketplace like &lt;a href="https://khetisetu.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;KhetiSetu&lt;/a&gt;, content is dynamic. We have static UI elements (buttons, labels) and dynamic data (crop names, variety names). &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For static UI, we use a dictionary pattern. Instead of a heavy library that loads everything on the client, we fetch only the necessary JSON on the server side using the &lt;code&gt;[locale]&lt;/code&gt; layout segment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The Directory Structure
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;app/
  [locale]/
    layout.tsx
    page.tsx
    mandi/
      page.tsx
dictionaries/
  en.json
  hi.json
  mr.json
  kn.json
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;We load these dictionaries using a simple asynchronous getter. This ensures that the Hindi translations aren't being sent to a user viewing the app in Kannada, keeping the payload small for users on spotty rural networks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Language&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Script&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Avg. Word Length (Relative)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Line Height Adjustment&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;English&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Latin&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.0x&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Standard&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Hindi&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Devanagari&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.2x&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+15% for Matras&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Marathi&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Devanagari&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.2x&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+15% for Matras&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Kannada&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Kannada&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.1x&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+20% for Ottu-aksharas&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The "Vernacular-First" UI Challenge
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the biggest mistakes engineers make is assuming that a &lt;code&gt;16px&lt;/code&gt; font size works across all scripts. Devanagari (Hindi/Marathi) and Kannada scripts have complex "matras" (diacritics) and "ottu-aksharas" (stacked consonants). &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you use a standard &lt;code&gt;line-height: 1.2&lt;/code&gt;, the top and bottom of the Kannada characters will often get clipped on mobile browsers. We had to implement script-specific CSS variables to handle this.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* globals.css */&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;hi&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;mr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nv"&gt;--base-font-size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;17px&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nv"&gt;--base-line-height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;1.6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;kn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nv"&gt;--base-font-size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;16px&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nv"&gt;--base-line-height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;1.8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.body-text&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;font-size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;--base-font-size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;line-height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;--base-line-height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Furthermore, "Translation" is often the wrong word. We focus on "Transcreation." For example, the word "Submit" doesn't have a direct, natural-sounding equivalent in a rural context. In Hindi, we use "आगे बढ़ें" (Move forward) or "जमा करें" (Deposit/Submit), depending on the action. Using overly formal Sanskritized Hindi makes the app feel like a government form rather than a tool for the community.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Handling Dynamic Data and SEO
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For an agri-tech marketplace, SEO is vital. If a farmer searches for "सोयाबीन भाव" (Soybean price) in Marathi, we need our Marathi-specific pages to rank. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Next.js makes this easier with &lt;code&gt;generateMetadata&lt;/code&gt;. We ensure that each page includes &lt;code&gt;hreflang&lt;/code&gt; tags, telling Google that the &lt;code&gt;/hi/&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;/mr/&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;/kn/&lt;/code&gt; versions of a page are equivalents.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;generateMetadata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;locale&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;baseUrl&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://khetisetu.com'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;alternates&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;canonical&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`${baseUrl}/${locale}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;languages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'hi-IN'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`${baseUrl}/hi`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'mr-IN'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`${baseUrl}/mr`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'kn-IN'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`${baseUrl}/kn`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  Performance: The 2-Second Rule
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Farmers are often using $100-150 Android phones. These devices have limited CPU power to parse large JavaScript bundles. By using Next.js Server Components, we move the localization logic—the heavy lifting of mapping keys to strings—to the server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The client receives the final HTML with the correct Hindi or Kannada strings already rendered. There is no "flash of un-localized text" (FOUT) where the user sees "SUBMIT_BUTTON" for half a second before it turns into "जमा करें".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Conclusion: Engineering for Trust
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building for the Indian agricultural sector requires a shift in mindset. You aren't just writing code; you're building a bridge across a digital divide. By utilizing Next.js's middleware and server-side rendering, we've managed to provide a seamless experience for Hindi, Marathi, and Kannada speakers on &lt;a href="https://khetisetu.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;KhetiSetu&lt;/a&gt; without sacrificing performance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Localization in agri-tech isn't about translating words; it's about respecting the user's language enough to make the technology invisible. When a farmer sees their local Mandi prices in their own script, the barrier to entry vanishes. And in the world of Indian agriculture, that is the ultimate engineering goal.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Docusaurus is Meta's open-source tool for building documentation websites, and it is the default choice for an enormous number of open-source projects. You write Markdown, and Docusaurus turns it into a fast, searchable, versioned docs site with navigation, dark mode, and a blog — all with almost no configuration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reason so many projects use it is simple: docs live next to the code, in Markdown, and the site builds itself. You do not design a docs site; you write docs, and Docusaurus handles the rest.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx create-docusaurus@latest my-docs classic
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cd&lt;/span&gt; my-docs
npm start
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A doc page is just Markdown with front matter:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight markdown"&gt;&lt;code&gt;---
title: "Getting Started"
sidebar_position: 1
---

&lt;span class="gu"&gt;# Getting Started
&lt;/span&gt;
Install the package, then run the command below.

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;
bash&lt;br&gt;
npm install my-package&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Docusaurus is a great fit for project documentation, API guides, tutorials, and any site where content is mostly Markdown. Versioning lets you keep docs for v1 and v2 side by side, and the built-in search works without a server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The limits are narrow. Docusaurus is a docs site generator, not a general app framework — if you need custom interactivity everywhere, you will fight it. Heavy customization of the theme takes real React work. And for a simple blog, a lighter tool may be enough.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But if you maintain an open-source project or a product with real documentation, start with Docusaurus. Your users will thank you, and your docs will finally stay up to date.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Gatsby is a React framework for building fast, content-driven websites — blogs, documentation sites, marketing pages, and e-commerce storefronts. It pulls data from anywhere (Markdown files, a CMS, an API), builds your pages at build time, and ships them as static HTML with React hydration. The result is a site that loads instantly and still feels like an app.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What makes Gatsby special is its data layer. Every piece of content becomes a node in a GraphQL graph, and every page queries exactly the data it needs. You stop writing fetch calls and start writing queries.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm init gatsby my-site
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cd&lt;/span&gt; my-site
npm run develop
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A page that queries Markdown posts looks like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;graphql&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'gatsby'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Blog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ul&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;allMarkdownRemark&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;nodes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;li&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;frontmatter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;li&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ul&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;graphql&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`
  query {
    allMarkdownRemark {
      nodes {
        id
        frontmatter { title }
      }
    }
  }
`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Gatsby is a great fit for blogs, docs, and marketing sites with lots of content. Its plugin ecosystem covers images, SEO, analytics, and CMS integrations, and its image component automatically optimizes every picture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The limits matter. Build times grow with content — a site with tens of thousands of pages can take a long time to build. And for apps with highly dynamic, user-specific data, a server-rendered framework like Next.js or SvelteKit is a better fit. Gatsby shines when content changes slowly and speed matters most.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your next project is a blog or docs site, try Gatsby for one section first. The moment you see a page load with zero spinners, you will understand the appeal.&lt;/p&gt;
